.\" Manpage for photon.
.\" Contact q.fang@neu.edu to correct errors or typos.
.TH PHOTON 1 "October 2026" "v2026 (3.0)" "photon man page"
.SH NAME
photon \- one command-line launcher for MCX, MCX-CL and MMC
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B photon
[\fB\-\-mcx\fR|\fB\-\-mcxcl\fR|\fB\-\-mmc\fR] [\fB\-\-which\fR] [\fB\-\-photon\-quiet\fR]
\fIoptions of mcx, mcxcl or mmc\fR ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Monte Carlo eXtreme (\fBmcx\fR), its OpenCL version (\fBmcxcl\fR) and Mesh-based
Monte Carlo (\fBmmc\fR) are GPU-accelerated 3-D photon transport simulators
developed at the Computational Optics and Translational Imaging (COTI) Lab,
Northeastern University, Boston, USA (https://mcx.space).
.PP
\fBphoton\fR takes the command line of any of the three, decides which
simulator the input is for, and runs that simulator with the same options: a
command starting with \fBmcx\fR, \fBmcxcl\fR or \fBmmc\fR can start with
\fBphoton\fR instead. The simulator replaces photon when it starts, so its
output and exit code are photon's.
.SH CHOOSING THE SIMULATOR
.TP
\fBmmc\fR
when the input describes a tetrahedral mesh: a JSON input (\fB\-f\fR file or
\fB\-\-json\fR fragment) with a \fBMesh\fR object, or with mesh data
(\fBMeshNode\fR, \fBMeshElem\fR or \fBMeshID\fR) in \fBShapes\fR; an .inp file in
mmc's layout (a mesh file stub on line 6, or a single integer on line 7); an
mmc-only option (\fB\-c\fR/\fB\-\-compute\fR, \fB\-C\fR/\fB\-\-basisorder\fR,
\fB\-M\fR/\fB\-\-method\fR, \fB\-\-gridsize\fR, \fB\-\-mc\fR); or
\fB\-\-bench\fR with a built-in benchmark only mmc has
.TP
\fBmcx\fR
for a voxel input, when \fBmcx \-L\fR finds an NVIDIA (CUDA) device
.TP
\fBmcxcl\fR
for a voxel input otherwise \- any OpenCL device, the CPU included \- or when an
OpenCL-only option is given (\fB\-J\fR/\fB\-\-compileopt\fR,
\fB\-o\fR/\fB\-\-optlevel\fR, \fB\-\-showkernel\fR)
.SH OPTIONS
All options other than the ones below are passed to the simulator unchanged,
\fB\-\-help\fR included.
.TP
\fB\-\-mcx\fR, \fB\-\-mcxcl\fR, \fB\-\-mmc\fR
run that simulator, whatever the input (\fB\-mcx\fR, \fB\-mcxcl\fR and
\fB\-mmc\fR also work)
.TP
\fB\-\-which\fR
print the simulator photon would run, its path and the reason, and exit
.TP
\fB\-\-photon\-quiet\fR
do not print photon's one-line note (written to standard error) before
running the simulator
.TP
\fB\-\-info\fR
print photon's help, including where each simulator was found
.SH FINDING THE SIMULATORS
Each simulator is looked for, in this order, in: the environment variable
\fBPHOTON_MCX\fR, \fBPHOTON_MCXCL\fR or \fBPHOTON_MMC\fR (a full path); the
folder photon is in; the MCX Suite and release layouts beside it
(\fI../mcx/bin\fR, \fI../../mcx/bin\fR, \fIMCXSuite/mcx/bin\fR and the same for
mcxcl and mmc); the \fBPATH\fR; \fI~/bin\fR; \fI/usr/libexec\fR and
\fI/usr/local/bin\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
photon \-\-bench cube60 \-n 1e7
mcx on an NVIDIA GPU, mcxcl otherwise
.TP
photon \-f brain_mesh.json
mmc, as the input carries a mesh
.TP
photon \-\-which \-f input.json
show which simulator would run, without running it
.TP
photon \-\-mcxcl \-\-bench cube60 \-G 1
mcxcl on its first device, even with an NVIDIA GPU present
.SH SEE ALSO
mcx(1), mcxcl(1), mmc(1), mcxstudio(1)
.SH AUTHOR
Qianqian Fang (q.fang@neu.edu)
